### 3. RK3506 I2C Controller Register Layout

**Source:** `/home/b4qaq/project/RK3506G2/rk3506_linux6.1_sdk_v1.2.0_iot_evm/kernel-6.1/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-rk3x.c`

The RK3506 uses the standard Rockchip "rk3x" I2C IP block. Compatible strings: `"rockchip,rk3506-i2c", "rockchip,rk3399-i2c"`.

#### Register Offsets

| Register | Offset | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `REG_CON` | `0x00` | Control register (enable, mode, start, stop, ack config) |
| `REG_CLKDIV` | `0x04` | Clock divisor register (div_high[31:16] \| div_low[15:0]) |
| `REG_MRXADDR` | `0x08` | Slave address for REGISTER_TX mode |
| `REG_MRXRADDR` | `0x0C` | Slave register address for REGISTER_TX mode |
| `REG_MTXCNT` | `0x10` | Number of bytes to transmit |
| `REG_MRXCNT` | `0x14` | Number of bytes to receive |
| `REG_IEN` | `0x18` | Interrupt enable register |
| `REG_IPD` | `0x1C` | Interrupt pending (write 1 to clear) |
| `REG_FCNT` | `0x20` | Finished byte count |
| `REG_SCL_OE_DB` | `0x24` | Slave hold SCL debounce |
| `REG_CON1` | `0x228` | Control register 1 (auto-stop features, v5+) |
| `TXBUFFER_BASE` | `0x100` | Transmit data buffer (8 x 32-bit words = 32 bytes) |
| `RXBUFFER_BASE` | `0x200` | Receive data buffer (8 x 32-bit words = 32 bytes) |

#### REG_CON Bit Definitions

| Bit(s) | Name | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `[0]` | `REG_CON_EN` | I2C controller enable |
| `[2:1]` | `REG_CON_MOD` | Transfer mode: 0=TX, 1=REGISTER_TX, 2=RX |
| `[3]` | `REG_CON_START` | Generate START condition |
| `[4]` | `REG_CON_STOP` | Generate STOP condition |
| `[5]` | `REG_CON_LASTACK` | 1 = send NACK after last received byte |
| `[6]` | `REG_CON_ACTACK` | 1 = stop if NACK is received |
| `[15:8]` | `REG_CON_TUNING_MASK` | SDA/STA/STO timing tuning |
| `[24:16]` | `REG_CON_VERSION` | Hardware version field |

#### REG_CON1 Bit Definitions (v5+ auto-stop)

| Bit | Name | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `[0]` | `REG_CON1_AUTO_STOP` | Enable auto-stop |
| `[1]` | `REG_CON1_TRANSFER_AUTO_STOP` | Auto-stop on transfer complete |
| `[2]` | `REG_CON1_NACK_AUTO_STOP` | Auto-stop on NACK |

#### Interrupt Flags (REG_IEN / REG_IPD)

| Bit | Name | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `[0]` | `REG_INT_BTF` | Byte transmitted |
| `[1]` | `REG_INT_BRF` | Byte received |
| `[2]` | `REG_INT_MBTF` | Master data transmit finished |
| `[3]` | `REG_INT_MBRF` | Master data receive finished |
| `[4]` | `REG_INT_START` | START condition generated |
| `[5]` | `REG_INT_STOP` | STOP condition generated |
| `[6]` | `REG_INT_NAKRCV` | NACK received |
| `[7]` | `REG_INT_SLV_HDSCL` | Slave hold SCL |

#### Transfer Modes

| Mode | Value | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `REG_CON_MOD_TX` | 0 | Transmit data |
| `REG_CON_MOD_REGISTER_TX` | 1 | Write slave addr + optional register addr, then restart |
| `REG_CON_MOD_RX` | 2 | Receive data |

#### Key Constraints

- **Max 32 bytes** per TX or RX chunk (buffer is 32 bytes at offset 0x100/0x200)
- For reads, the hardware first transmits the slave address using `MOD_REGISTER_TX` mode, then switches to `MOD_RX`
- For SMBus-style register reads (write reg_addr then read), the hardware can combine both via `REG_MRXADDR` / `REG_MRXRADDR` when write len < 4

